  "markdown_report": "At 0% progress, prioritize a small, testable architectural change: **zero-initialized recent-history pooling alongside SASRec**, accompanied by controlled history diagnostics. No improvement is established yet.\n\n**Insights from the starting point.** (1) Feedback identifies broad relatedness crowding out specific next choices, motivating a recency experiment without establishing its cause. (2) Training margins cannot establish validation gains or useful history dependence. (3) Compatibility and redundancy claims require explicit evidence; histories cannot establish ownership or continuing demand.\n\n**Insights from related work.** (1) [Recency research](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.09722) cautions against excessive recent-item emphasis. (2) [FCESR](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.13632) distinguishes sufficiency from necessity, motivating matched retention/deletion probes without equating dependence with intent. (3) [Krichene–Rendle](https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/) establish sampled-ranking inconsistencies; retain the pinned metric and qualify broader claims. (4) [SASRec](https://github.com/kang205/SASRec/blob/master/model.py) and [ReZero](https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.04887) motivate preserving embedding scales and using exact baseline-equivalent initialization.\n\n**Framework and directions.**\n\n| Layer | Existing approach | Proposed addition | Remaining gap |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Prediction | SASRec sequence features | Zero-initialized recent residual | Recent activity may be incidental |\n| Dependence | Training margins | Matched deletion and retention | Dependence does not establish intent |\n| Interpretation | Metadata breakdowns | Source-supported flags and abstention | Ownership and prerequisites remain uncertain |\n\n**Candidate ideas and assessments.** Scores are originality/potential/difficulty; higher difficulty means harder implementation.\n\n1. **Recent-history residual — 3/6/3.** Originality: inspectable combination, but established components. Potential: cheap and ablatable, but may duplicate SASRec. Difficulty: small change, but export parity matters.\n2. **Matched history perturbations — 4/6/4.** Originality: task-specific controls, but established methods. Potential: exposes dependence, but does not improve ranking directly. Difficulty: hooks suffice, but positional effects require care.\n3. **Evidence-backed rejection flags — 5/6/6.** Originality: connects feedback to spans, but resembles rule-based verification. Potential: actionable diagnosis, but limited coverage. Difficulty: feasible extraction, but brittle specification matching.\n\n**Selected algorithm.** Compute baseline features h and the causal, non-padding weighted mean p of the last k=5 input item embeddings, decay=0.7. Use z=h+αp, initializing learned per-dataset α=0. Preserve baseline embedding scales, loss, optimizer and seeds. Unlike the previous sigmoid(-4) plus extra LayerNorm proposal, this preserves initial scores exactly; mixing strength still depends on feature norms.\n\n```python\nh = sasrec(sequence)\np = causal_recent_pool(item_emb(sequence), k=5, decay=0.7)\nz = mask_padding(h + alpha * p)  # alpha starts at zero\nlogits = baseline_candidate_dot(z, item_emb(candidates))\n```\n\nCompare unchanged baseline and residual first; conditionally examine normalization-only, pooling-only and combined variants. The former sigmoid(-4)/extra-normalization design remains a comparator, not an equivalent initialization. Uniform pooling, last-item, raw/√d-scaled pools, fixed/learned gates and scalar-temperature controls are follow-ups subject to budget. Log norms, gates, gradients and logit spread. Examine popularity/history-length slices and matched perturbations for shortcuts; distinguish inference sensitivity from retrained ablations.\n\nPredeclare checkpoint rules, validation frequency and trial limits; select only pinned macro validation HR@10. Paired within-category bootstrap intervals exclude training variation and selection bias. Preserve all four routes, splits, seeds, DATA_DIR and trial-local artifacts. Retain DiagnosisProbe’s interface, actual model reference, residual-enhanced features and consistent scoring; unavailable category lists remain empty. Bound diagnostics, retain twenty-user full-catalog recommendations, and verify exported scores/rankings. Treat simulator feedback and repeatedly selected validation gains as exploratory.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Add a zero-initialized learned residual from causal recent-history embedding pooling to each SASRec sequence feature, independently for all four dataset models. Replace the originally proposed sigmoid gate and extra LayerNorm in the primary experiment so initialization exactly preserves baseline scores.",
    "motivation": "Qualitative feedback motivates testing whether recent observed activity supplies additional predictive evidence, but it does not establish recency as the cause of failures. A minimal residual permits a controlled experiment. Zero initialization avoids confounding the intervention with a fresh normalization layer or immediate score changes; it does not guarantee improved ranking or intent alignment.",
    "implementation_notes": "Retain DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, evaluation seed 42, all four dataset routes and unchanged input splits/item mappings. Respect SASREC_EPOCHS=200 from the environment and baseline overrides. Use k=5 and decay=0.7 initially; alpha is one unconstrained scalar per dataset, initialized to zero, and may become negative. Pool raw item embeddings before positional embeddings/dropout; preserve the baseline's existing sequence-input scaling, candidate embeddings, final normalization and scoring. Do not insert an additional LayerNorm in the primary variant. Pool only events at or before each prediction position, with zero-padding masks and no wraparound. Because normalized pooling averages vectors, its norm can be much smaller than the sequence feature norm; record pool/feature norm ratios, alpha, alpha gradients, effective residual norms and logit spread. A zero alpha initially blocks residual-branch embedding gradients but permits learning alpha when its loss gradient is nonzero. Preserve baseline model initialization and RNG consumption: add the scalar without random initialization, reuse identical baseline weights for initial equivalence checks, and use separate diagnostic RNG streams. First compare unchanged baseline against the residual under matched training settings and tuning effort; alpha fixed at zero is an implementation equivalence control, not evidence of a separate mechanism. If justified by remaining budget, compare LN_extra(h), h+g*p and LN_extra(h+g*p), including the former sigmoid gate initialized at -4. Its initial gate is about 0.018, and extra LayerNorm is not baseline-equivalent. Raw versus sqrt(d)-scaled pools, fixed versus learned gates, and baseline score-temperature controls distinguish representation effects from loss-scale effects. Include last-item and uniform/decayed pooling controls with matched embeddings and loss before claiming higher-order sequence benefits. Preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device) and run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), returning metrics and metric_definitions. view.model must be the actual trained nn.Module; view.item_emb, log2feats and predict_score must use the exported architecture. Retain user_train, meta_data and review_data compatibility fields without inventing unavailable reviews, ratings, categories, prices, stores or traits. Preserve existing embedding and margin diagnostics with their limitations; unavailable category breakdowns remain empty. Bound probes by fixed batches/users/catalog chunks and runtime caps, retain required full-catalog unseen-item recommendations for five training users per category, and record supporting public text/history spans for any compatibility or redundancy flags. Unknown prerequisites remain unknown; diagnostic abstention must not suppress recommendations. For perturbations, match deletion counts, account for padding and position shifts, and separate inference-time sensitivity from retrained controls. Use training-only popularity and history-length thresholds; report slice sizes and paired validation changes without selecting by slices. Predeclare validation cadence, checkpoint eligibility, patience, deterministic ties and a small trial plan; use baseline rules where available. Preserve the host's fixed validation users and candidates, and record per-user hit indicators when available without replacing host evaluation. Bootstrap paired differences within each category using a fixed seed and equally average category differences; intervals condition on trained models and fixed candidates and do not correct repeated-selection bias. Record all trials, validation queries, effective configuration, code and precision/backend settings. Save weights, architecture, optimizer/scheduler where present, and RNG states for resumption under the trial directory. Charge training, failures and diagnostics against the aggregate 43,200 GPU-second budget and reserve export-check time. Update the predictor writer to serialize the exact architecture. Reload through exported inference and compare scores using predeclared precision-appropriate tolerances, top-10 rankings and validation HR@10; investigate ranking differences rather than dismissing them as numerical noise. Check padding, truncation, masks, item IDs and routing. No validation improvement, causal intent matching or full-catalog benefit is established in advance.",
    "pseudocode": "class RecentResidualSASRec(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, baseline):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.sasrec = baseline\n        self.alpha = nn.Parameter(torch.zeros(()))\n        # Access item_emb through baseline; avoid duplicate module registration.\n\n    @property\n    def item_emb(self):\n        return self.sasrec.item_emb\n\n    def log2feats(self, sequence):\n        h = self.sasrec.log2feats(sequence)\n        embeddings = self.item_emb(sequence)\n        numerator = zeros_like(embeddings)\n        denominator = zeros_like(sequence, dtype=embeddings.dtype)\n        # shift_right inserts zeros and never wraps around.\n        for offset in range(5):\n            shifted_ids = shift_right(sequence, offset)\n            shifted_emb = shift_right(embeddings, offset)\n            weight = (0.7 ** offset) * (shifted_ids != 0)\n            numerator += shifted_emb * weight[..., None]\n            denominator += weight\n        recent = numerator / denominator.clamp_min(1)[..., None]\n        features = h + self.alpha * recent\n        return features * (sequence != 0)[..., None]\n\n    def predict_score(self, features, items):\n        # Preserve baseline candidate broadcasting, scaling and any scoring terms.\n        return self.sasrec.predict_score(features, items)\n\n# Ensure forward/training uses this wrapper's log2feats, not baseline.forward.\n# Train with the unchanged baseline loss and optimizer, including alpha.\n# Instantiate four independent dataset models; preserve dataset routing.\n# Before training: copy baseline weights, set eval mode, assert matching\n# non-padding features and candidate scores at alpha=0.\n# Select only with the pinned macro validation HR@10 and declared checkpoint rules.\n# Export the exact wrapper/configuration through the predictor writer.\n# Reload selected checkpoints and verify scores, rankings, masks and routing.\n# Keep diagnostics bounded and record all GPU time against the shared budget.",
